AB  - Brown adipose tissue (BAT) is a potential therapeutic target for cardiometabolic diseases and is activated by β-adrenergic receptor (β-AR) signaling. Two randomized, single-blind, placebo-controlled crossover trials examined responses to non-selective intravenous β-AR stimulation in healthy adults, with co-administration of an oral β3-AR agonist (mirabegron; Study 1) or an intravenous antioxidant (vitamin C; Study 2). Placebo data were pooled (n = 28, 14 M/14F). Thermogenesis was assessed (graded isoproterenol infusion, 6, 12, and 24 ng/kg fat-free mass/min, 30 min each). Energy expenditure and supraclavicular and reference-region skin temperatures were measured using indirect calorimetry and infrared thermography (IRT), respectively. Baseline energy expenditure was 1.06 ± 0.26 kcal/min. Isoproterenol increased energy expenditure at each dose (0.09 [0.05, 0.13], 0.13 [0.09, 0.18], and 0.20 [0.13, 0.26] kcal/min; mean differences [95% CIs]; all p < 0.001). Supraclavicular skin warming was not regionally specific: although it warmed at the highest dose (0.56°C [0.24, 0.88], p < 0.001), the sternum warmed more, exceeding it by 0.70°C [0.28, 1.12] (p < 0.001). Mirabegron increased energy expenditure (0.07 [0.04, 0.10] kcal/min, p < 0.001) without raising supraclavicular skin temperature (p = 0.29); vitamin C did not augment thermogenesis. Isoproterenol increased whole-body thermogenesis, but supraclavicular skin IRT did not isolate a BAT-specific thermal signal. Supraclavicular skin warming alone should be interpreted cautiously as evidence of β-AR-mediated BAT activation under room-temperature conditions.
UR  - https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/42995650/
