ID  - 42898555
TI  - Global Warming and Type 2 Diabetes: Sex and Gender as Drivers in a Multidimensional Feedback Loop.
AU  - D'Archivio Massimo
AU  - Zanchi Giulia
AU  - Tammaro Alessia
AU  - Masella Roberta
AU  - Cataldo Claudia
JO  - Global challenges (Hoboken, NJ)
PY  - 2026
DO  - 10.1002/gch2.70140
AB  - Climate change is increasingly recognized as a determinant of metabolic health. Emerging evidence indicates that rising temperatures impair glucose homeostasis-specifically through reduced brown adipose tissue activity and increased insulin resistance-thereby exacerbating type 2 diabetes mellitus (T2DM) risk. Concurrently, food systems drive substantial greenhouse gas emissions (GHGE), with gender-differentiated dietary patterns uniquely influencing both environmental impact and health outcomes. In this perspective, we move beyond well-established separate links to propose an original integrated and systemic conceptual framework. This framework connects gender-related drivers in dietary behaviors, GHGE, global warming, brown adipose tissue dysfunction, and T2DM risk into an integrated multidimensional feedback model, providing a novel basis to generate hypotheses for future mechanistic, epidemiological, and intervention studies, supporting gender-sensitive prevention strategies.
UR  - https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/42898555/
